{"text": " AI engineer versus ML engineer. They sound like the same job, they are not. At the top, the pay gap between them is huge. One of these roles gets the big checks, the other one gets the door open. So let's break it all down. The AI engineer is commonly associated with shipping products. On a model, someone else has already trained. The ML engineer is commonly associated with the model itself, billing it, training it and deploying it. Day to day, that means wiring a model like Claude into your data and your product and then testing whether the answers are any good. That's my side. I've run Claude agents across whole pipelines and I never actually trained a model. So why does an AI engineer even exist? Because the hard part, the training, is already done for you. But as soon as that API bill gets too big, you can and should post and tune your own model, which is something that I've been thinking about lately. Hmm, how to cut those costs. To sum it up, AI engineering is less about developing models and more about adapting and evaluating them. If you have the research background, the stats, the math, the graduate degree, go for ML. If you're a software developer, choose AI. If you wanna train models, ML. If you wanna ship products, AI. Think of shipping a support chat bot on Claude versus training a fraud model. And right now, LinkedIn's fastest growing job in the US for 2026 is AI engineer. If you have the code but not the math, AI is your way in. If you have the math but not the production code, here's the thing, ML teams want shipping experience too. Now the money, in total pay, the typical engineer in the US makes around $155,000 a year. And the top 10% of ML engineers start at around $495,000 a year. My God, dude, that's a $340,000 gap. And it goes to the people who train the models. But those seats are few and you're competing with PhDs for them, something to ponder. Therefore, for most developers, AI engineering is the key. The door is open, you already have the skills. So subscribe for more steady content on how to become an AI engineer. All my content on this channel is geared to help you on your AI engineering journey. And watch this next video right here, or maybe even this one, or this one, or even this one.", "segments": [{"id": 0, "start": 0.0, "end": 2.58, "text": "AI engineer versus ML engineer.", "words": [{"word": " AI", "start": 0.0, "end": 0.46, "probability": 0.6338268518447876}, {"word": " engineer", "start": 0.46, "end": 1.06, "probability": 0.8024829030036926}, {"word": " versus", "start": 1.06, "end": 1.6, "probability": 0.5927250385284424}, {"word": " ML", "start": 1.6, "end": 2.02, "probability": 0.6750748753547668}, {"word": " engineer.", "start": 2.02, "end": 2.58, "probability": 0.9691144227981567}]}, {"id": 1, "start": 2.9, "end": 5.2, "text": "They sound like the same job, they are not.", "words": [{"word": " They", "start": 2.9, "end": 2.9, "probability": 0.9701125621795654}, {"word": " sound", "start": 2.9, "end": 3.1, "probability": 0.9896001815795898}, {"word": " like", "start": 3.1, "end": 3.34, "probability": 0.9955101013183594}, {"word": " the", "start": 3.34, "end": 3.54, "probability": 0.990044116973877}, {"word": " same", "start": 3.54, "end": 3.74, "probability": 0.9991520643234253}, {"word": " job,", "start": 3.74, "end": 4.22, "probability": 0.9959840774536133}, {"word": " they", "start": 4.32, "end": 4.66, "probability": 0.9763363599777222}, {"word": " are", "start": 4.66, "end": 4.96, "probability": 0.9274865984916687}, {"word": " not.", "start": 4.96, "end": 5.2, "probability": 0.996769905090332}]}, {"id": 2, "start": 5.3, "end": 8.14, "text": "At the top, the pay gap between them is huge.", "words": [{"word": " At", "start": 5.3, "end": 5.54, "probability": 0.9921231865882874}, {"word": " the", "start": 5.54, "end": 5.76, "probability": 0.9992573857307434}, {"word": " top,", "start": 5.76, "end": 6.08, "probability": 0.9924570322036743}, {"word": " the", "start": 6.3, "end